    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d17">

                <pb facs="0107" n="39" sameAs="frus:frus1867p1/pg39"/>

                <head>Mr. Adams to Mr.
                        Seward.</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 1297.]</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">Legation of the United States</hi>,
                                <hi rendition="#italic">London,</hi></placeName>
                        <date calendar="gregorian" when="1867-01-02"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >January</hi> 2<hi rendition="#italic">,</hi>
                        1867</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir:</hi> At the particular request of Mr. Morse, I
                    transmit herewith a statement made by him in answer to the extraordinary
                    deposition of Mr. Hull, constituting a part of the proceedings in the suit with
                    Mr. Prioleau. The more obvious and proper course would seem to have been to
                    prepare a deposition to be used, if necessary, in a later stage of the case
                    itself. I have taken the liberty to suggest this to Mr. Morse, in order that his
                    statement may be as fully accessible on the record as Mr. Hull’s, to which it
                    replies.</p>
                <p>I have the honor to be, sir, your obedient servant,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>CHARLES FRANCIS ADAMS.</signed>
                </closer>
                <postscript>
                    <p>Hon. <hi rendition="#small-caps">William H. Seward</hi>, <hi
                            rendition="#italic">Secretary of State, Washington, D. C.</hi></p>
                </postscript>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
